{{!< default}}

{{!-- The homepage Surface, in whichever layout the publication picked.

THE LAYOUT PICKER PATTERN — every later Surface repeats this shape, so it is worth reading
once rather than re-deciding:

  1. One {{#match}} on the setting, at the top of the template, dispatching to one partial
	 per layout. Nothing below reads @custom, so a partial can be moved or shared without
	 dragging a settings dependency with it.
  2. The NON-DEFAULT option is the one matched explicitly, and the default lives in the
	 {{else}}. That is what makes an absent or unrecognised value safe: it cannot match, so
	 it falls through and renders a layout rather than nothing. Ghost validates a stored
	 value against the declared options, but a value stored before an option was renamed
	 survives in the database — this is the case that would otherwise render a blank page.
  3. Data is fetched once, above the dispatch, and both layouts consume the same block
	 param. Two layouts must never mean two queries.
  4. A child partial that varies by layout takes it as an argument
	 ({{> "post-meta" author=true}}), the way Source threads navigationLayout — it never
	 reaches back up to @custom.

The dispatch is the {{#match}} here; the query and the two layouts are home/window.hbs, one
level down, and the layout reaches it as an argument. Rule 3 is why the query is not repeated in
the two branches: `{{> "home/window"}}` is one query definition, whichever branch calls it.

WHY THE PAGINATION IS HERE AND NOT IN THE LAYOUT. {{pagination}} reads its numbers off the
current context, and the {{#get}} in home/window.hbs renders its block with the query's own
response as the context — a control inside it would offer nine pages of twelve on a Surface that
has six pages of eighteen. Called here it reads the page's own pagination. (`@root` would rebind
it in place, and gscan rejects `@root` as an unknown global, so the structure carries it
instead.)

`pagination.pages` guards the call because the helper throws outside a paginated context: with
the layout in docs/routes.example.yaml this Surface IS page 1 of the `/` collection and pages
through it, but a publication that hands `/` to a `route:` has no pagination at all, and one
that has fewer posts than a page has no second page to offer. The editorial slots are indifferent
to all three, which is what the {{#get}} in home/window.hbs buys.

A2 narrows its article column to 1120px and leaves the blocks below it — the subscribe band and
the writers strip — at page width. The override is a wrapper here because it has to contain the
pagination too: `--container-max` is what every `container` reads, and the control belongs to the
grid above it rather than to the page. --}}

{{> "chrome/header"}}

<main
	id="main"
	class="grow"
	data-surface="home">

	{{#match @custom.homepage_layout "Editorial masthead"}}
		<div style="--container-max:1120px">
			{{> "home/window" wide=true}}

			{{#if pagination.pages}}
				{{pagination pt="pt-14"}}
			{{/if}}
		</div>
	{{else}}
		{{> "home/window"}}

		{{#if pagination.pages}}
			{{pagination pt="pt-14"}}
		{{/if}}
	{{/match}}

	{{> "newsletter" anchor="newsletter" pt="pt-16"}}

	{{> "writers" anchor="authors" headingId="au-head"}}

</main>

{{> "chrome/footer"}}
